\strong{Cumulative} mirrors the cascade used by \code{\link[=cohort]{cohort()}}, \code{\link[=excluded]{excluded()}} and the
CONSORT diagram, and is built from the recorded steps without re-evaluating
anything. Each row has at most one \code{FALSE}. \code{NA} means the row never
reached that criterion, which is not the same as failing it; it only
becomes \code{FALSE} if you ask for that with \code{na = "false"}. Missing predicate
values follow the cascade: an \code{include()} that evaluates to \code{NA} removes
the row (\code{FALSE}), an \code{exclude()} that evaluates to \code{NA} keeps it (\code{TRUE}).

\strong{Independent} evaluates every criterion against the full starting data,
ignoring earlier steps, so a row can fail several criteria. Bear in mind:
\itemize{
\item \code{group_include()}, \code{group_exclude()} and \code{select_within()} depend on
which rows are in each group. Evaluated on the full data, a group can
pass or fail differently than it did in the cascade, where earlier steps
had already removed some of its rows.
\item In a \code{\link[=continue_criteria]{continue_criteria()}} flow, columns joined at a later stage are \code{NA}
for rows excluded before the join, so later-stage predicates are usually
\code{NA} for those rows.
\item \code{randomise()} steps are all \code{TRUE} (in cumulative mode they are \code{TRUE}
where reached and \code{NA} otherwise). They are dropped by default.
}
